{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00389 - MONDO_4425",
  "verbalization": "Carbimazole is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hyperthyroidism through a series of mechanistic links that converge on the condition. The evidence suggests that Carbimazole is associated with Thyrotoxicosis, a clinical manifestation of hyperthyroidism, and that Thyrotoxicosis is linked to Methimazole, a drug with a known mode of action similar to Carbimazole. This creates a pathway where Carbimazole may contribute to the development of Hyperthyroidism via the induction of Thyrotoxicosis. Additionally, Carbimazole is linked to Graves Disease, a common cause of Thyrotoxicosis, further reinforcing the potential role of Carbimazole in the development of Hyperthyroidism. The evidence is supported by a consistent clinical and pharmacological relationship, with the core mechanism involving the inhibition of thyroid hormone synthesis. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of Thyrotoxicosis as a central mediator in the relationship between Carbimazole and Hyperthyroidism.",
